<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/9th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Versinikia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/813">813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Versinicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Versinikia was fought on 22 June 813 near Versinikia, not far from Adrianople in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Khan Krum</a> routed the Byzantine army of Emperor Michael I Rangabe, whose defeat led to his overthrow in favour of Leo V the Armenian. Krum went on to besiege Adrianople and appeared before the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-versinikia.jpg" alt="Battle of Versinikia" width="320" height="301" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kandi · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Territorial_expansion_during_the_reign_of_Khan_Krum_(803-814).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a>'s victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a> at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a> in 811, the emperor's son Staurakios was left paralysed, and on 2 October 811 senior officials, among them Patriarch Nikephoros I, forced him to abdicate in favour of his brother-in-law Michael I Rangabe. Bulgaria resumed its attacks the next year in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Struma</a> valley. In the autumn of 812 a Bulgarian delegation led by Dobromir offered peace, but Michael refused, possibly over the clause of the Treaty of 716 on handing back refugees. Krum then took Mesembria with siege machines built by an emigrant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, capturing 36 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> siphons for throwing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-fire">Greek fire</a> and much gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>.</p><p>When Bulgarian scouting raids in February 813 pulled back after several clashes, Michael took the retreat for a victory and called for a counter-attack. He summoned troops from every theme, including the guards of the Syrian passes, and after delays caused by unrest in the army left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in May, seen off outside the walls by the people of the city and Empress Prokopia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A solar eclipse on 4 May shook the soldiers' morale, and the army plundered settlements around Adrianople. In June the two armies camped close together near the fortress of Versinikia; John Skylitzes says the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> were ten times as many. After thirteen days of waiting, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of Macedonia, John Aplakes, attacked with the right wing and pushed back the Bulgarian left, but the centre and left did not follow.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> sent Slavic infantry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> horse archers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> against Aplakes, who was killed with most of his men. The Anatolian theme withdrew first, then the whole army, with Michael and Leo the Armenian among the first commanders to go. The later chroniclers Genesius and Theophanes Continuatus blamed Leo for ordering the flight; J. B. Bury and Steven Runciman agree, while Vasil Zlatarski disputes it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Michael withdrew to a monastery. On 17 July 813 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Krum</a> camped before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, sacrificed to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangra">Tangra</a> and offered peace. During the talks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> shot at his delegation, killing the kavkhan; in reply Krum destroyed the churches, monasteries and palaces outside the walls and razed fortresses around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>. He forced Adrianople to surrender and resettled 10,000 people north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. The site of Versinikia is unknown, though an inscription of Krum's reign found at Malomirovo describes the Bulgarian army's dispositions in 813.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Versinikia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
